## Why This Is Asked
This question blends BFS reachability, Dijkstra shortest paths, and counting shortest paths via a DAG and topological order.

## Key Concepts
- BFS/DFS reachability
- Dijkstra's algorithm
- Shortest-path DAG
- Topological DP and modular counting

## Code Example
```javascript
function countShortestPaths(n, adj, s) {
  const dist = Array(n).fill(Infinity); dist[s] = 0;
  // priority queue based Dijkstra (pseudo-notation)
  const pq = new MinPQ((a,b)=>a[0]-b[0]);
  pq.enqueue([0,s]);
  while(!pq.isEmpty()){
    const [d,u] = pq.dequeue(); if (d!==dist[u]) continue;
    for (const {to:v, w} of adj[u]){
      if (dist[v] > d + w){ dist[v] = d + w; pq.enqueue([dist[v], v]); }
    }
  }
  const dag = Array.from({length:n}, ()=>[]);
  for (let u=0; u<n; u++) for (const {to:v, w} of adj[u]) if (dist[u] + w === dist[v]) dag[u].push(v);
  const order = Array.from({length:n}, (_,i)=>i).sort((a,b)=>dist[a]-dist[b]);
  const MOD = 1000000007; const ways = Array(n).fill(0); ways[s] = 1;
  for (const u of order) for (const v of dag[u]) ways[v] = (ways[v] + ways[u]) % MOD;
  return {dist, ways};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would zero-weight edges affect the approach? 
- How to extend to count modulo a large prime or handle multiple sources?

## Why This Question Is Asked
This question tests dynamic programming intuition on a constrained path counting problem with simple transitions and boundary conditions.

## Key Concepts
- **Recurrence relation:** f(i) = f(i+1) + f(i+2) with modulo arithmetic
- **Memoization vs. tabulation:** Trade-offs between recursive top-down with caching versus iterative bottom-up approaches
- **Edge conditions:** Blocked start/end positions, single-cell arrays

## Code Example
```javascript
function countPaths(blocked) {
  const n = blocked.length;
  const MOD = 1000000007;
  if (blocked[0]) return 0;

  // Memoized DFS
  const memo = Array(n).fill(-1);
  function dfs(i) {
    if (i === n - 1) return blocked[i] ? 0 : 1;
    if (i >= n || blocked[i]) return 0;
    if (memo[i] !== -1) return memo[i];
    memo[i] = (dfs(i + 1) + dfs(i + 2)) % MOD;
    return memo[i];
  }
  return dfs(0);
}

// Bottom-up DP (O(1) space)
function countPathsDP(blocked) {
  const n = blocked.length;
  const MOD = 1000000007;
  if (blocked[0]) return 0;

  let next2 = 1, next1 = blocked[n - 1] ? 0 : 1;
  for (let i = n - 2; i >= 0; i--) {
    const curr = blocked[i] ? 0 : (next1 + next2) % MOD;
    next2 = next1;
    next1 = curr;
  }
  return next1;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a real-time aggregated leaderboard with sliding windows and per-key deduplication. It combines hash tables, heaps, and lazy expiration techniques to achieve O(log K) top-K retrieval while maintaining constant-time lookups.

## Key Concepts
- **Sliding window by timestamp**: Only events within the last W seconds contribute to the sum
- **Per-city deduplication**: Within each city, only the latest event per (city, driver_id) counts—older events from the same driver are discarded
- **City sums**: Maintained via per-city hashmaps and a max-heap for efficient ranking
- **Global top-K**: Implemented with a min-heap supporting O(log K) retrieval per update

